WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.520
Nuclear fusion has been a pipedream for decades.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.980
Always 20 years away.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:06.980
Never 19.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:13.330
It’s easy to get jaded about this technology
and write it off as impossible, especially

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.810
when nuclear fission energy already exists
and is being underutilised.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:24.330
But, by the end of this video, I hope that
I can change that feeling and get you as excited

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:28.490
as I am about the potential of this technology.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:34.820
If we, as a civilization, actually pull it
off and invent a cost effective nuclear fusion

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.910
power plant it would change the face of society.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:47.010
Clean safe fuels will allow every country
in the world to benefit from this technology.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.060
Allowing countries around the world to be
energy independent, preventing one of the

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:57.140
leading causes of conflicts around the world,
as we fight for control of energy sources.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:04.430
Cheap, reliable and abundant energy is the
foundation of every sci-fi utopian society.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.100
It would solve our issues with climate change.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.210
Allow us to electrify industries that require
fossil fuels, like steel smelting.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:17.480
Allow us to create entirely new industries
that have been held back by energy costs,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.420
like water desalination.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.740
Providing the world with fresh clean water
to irrigate our lands and turn barren wastelands

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.460
to fertile pastures.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:32.689
Ushering in an era of clean, safe abundance,
a utopian future that has been dreamed of

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:34.500
for decades.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.619
Nuclear fusion experiments have been underway
since the very earliest years of the cold

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:46.400
war, with the first generators firing up in
the 1950s in both the USA and USSR.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.990
The Soviet Union approached the problem with
a Tokamak design, while the Americans used

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.880
a slightly different approach, the stellarator.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.110
Each design attempts to solve the same problem.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.490
Fusion in essence isn’t terribly complicated.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.840
We can make new elements by combining smaller
elements, and in the process release a lot

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.040
of energy.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.920
However, to successfully combine elements
we need to overcome the electromagnetic repulsion

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:13.920
that pushes them apart.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.760
Like pushing two north poles of a magnet together,
atoms will repel each other.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:24.480
In order to force them together we need to
input a tremendous amount of energy, but we

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.150
can’t just grab individual atoms and force
them together like magnets.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:35.599
Instead, we create a plasma, essentially a
cloud of changed ions which, thanks to their

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:39.280
charge, can be manipulated by a magnetic field.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.129
We can then confine the plasma within a magnetic
field preventing the ions from hitting the

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:49.590
fusion generator walls, and gradually raise
its temperature to extremely high temperatures

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.810
that would otherwise melt every solid material
in the universe.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:03:00.010
Raising the temperature of the plasma causes
the ions to move faster and faster.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.370
Raising the ions' kinetic energy so high that
their speed alone allows them to punch through

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.190
the electromagnetic repulsion and collide.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:14.900
Both of these designs, the Tokamak and Stellator,
use slightly different methods of magnetic

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:21.220
field confinement, generated by massive superconducting
magnets, to achieve fusion.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:26.680
The Tokamak became the leading design today
as a result of a release of information from

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:34.569
the USSR on the tokamak design in 1968, which
showed a tremendous jump in energy efficiency.

00:03:34.569 --> 00:03:38.430
However, both designs used the same fuels.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.650
The exact reactants we use have a huge effect
on how much energy we need to put in, and

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.000
what we get out at the end.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.390
Most reactions use two isotopes of hydrogen.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:53.980
A regular run of the mill hydrogen has one
proton in its nucleus with one electron in

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:54.980
orbit.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:59.500
We could perform fusion with this kind of
hydrogen, but the energy we can extract out

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.210
of the reaction is very low.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.060
Instead we frequently combine deuterium and
tritium together.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.829
Where hydrogen normally has one proton and
one electron and no neutrons, deuterium has

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:17.940
one proton, one electron and one neutron,
while tritium has one proton and one neutron

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.900
and two neutron.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.690
This combination is used for a couple of reasons.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:28.259
First, it has the largest probability of giving
us the exact result we want.

00:04:28.259 --> 00:04:33.460
Other reactions, like a regular hydrogen to
hydrogen reaction, have a very high probability

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:40.540
of creating Helium 2, which is unstable and
almost instantly decays into 2 regular hydrogens

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.750
again, and releases very little energy in
the process.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.750
[2]

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.320
They have a lower probability of combining
to form deuterium, the reaction we actually

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.320
want.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:56.199
Which then go on to fuse to form helium 3
and finally helium 4.

00:04:56.199 --> 00:05:02.139
This is the reaction chain that powers the
Sun, but the Sun has an unfathomable amount

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:08.560
of particles making the probability issue
completely irrelevant AND the crushing gravity

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.570
needed to create the conditions needed for
fusion.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:18.000
We need to supply those particles and the
energy needed to combine them ourselves, and

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.169
if we can’t extract more energy than we
put in, that’s just a science experiment,

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:25.050
not an energy source.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:31.790
We have successfully created many many fusion
reactions here on Earth, in fact, I witnessed

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:37.039
the bright pink flashes of fusion myself while
visiting Helion recently.

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:39.160
We know how to achieve fusion.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.740
The problem we are now trying to solve is
lowering the energy we need to input, while

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.419
maximising the energy we can extract.

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:52.620
So, step 1, we need fuels that require less
energy input that release more energy.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.100
That’s where deuterium and tritium come
in.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:02.410
When combined they have a very high probability
of creating helium 4, and release on average

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:08.630
17.6 Mega Electronvolts (MeV) for each and
every fusion event.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:15.310
For comparison, Uranium 235 produces about
11.4 times this energy for each fission event

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:21.530
(200 MeV), but on a mass basis, that deuterium
tritium fusion reaction releases over four

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:28.440
times as much energy as uranium fission, and
produces no dangerous radioactive products.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:34.220
Helium is actually quite a useful byproduct,
being used to cool MRI machines’ superconducting

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:38.620
magnets, to fill rocket tanks after their
propellant has been expended to prevent them

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:44.380
from exploding, and occasionally to make your
voice sound like Wendover Productions 6 years

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.380
ago.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:49.580
Followed by this voice clip: https://youtu.be/6Oe8T3AvydU?t=539

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:54.350
And we will eventually run out of the gas,
so having a way to make it ourselves would

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.500
be a nice back up.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:00.530
Deuterium is fairly common on earth, occurring
naturally in seawater.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:04.569
Making up about 0.02% of hydrogen in seawater.

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:09.759
And because deuterium has an extra neutron,
it makes that water molecule heavier, giving

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:10.970
it its name.

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.539
Heavy Water.

00:07:12.539 --> 00:07:16.620
That difference allows us to separate it through
a number of means.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:22.110
Vacuum distillation allows us to take advantage
of heavy water's higher boiling point, while

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:27.280
the girdler sulphide process separates heavy
water through chemical reactions.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.250
We can then simply electrolyse the heavy water
to separate the deuterium

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:36.970
However, one of the issues facing nuclear
fusion is the rarity of tritium.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:42.210
Our primary source of tritium is nuclear reactor
moderator pools, which are often filled with

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.680
heavy water.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:49.610
These pools are designed to absorb the high
energy neutrons given off during nuclear fission,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.139
and in doing so can become tritium.

00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:54.190
A hydrogen with 2 neutrons.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:55.190
[5]

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:59.449
This source of tritium is becoming less prevalent
as nuclear power plants are gradually being

00:07:59.449 --> 00:08:05.140
shut down around the world due to competition
from cheaper forms of electricity.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:10.491
Currently total global reserves of tritium
is estimated at just twenty kilograms, which

00:08:10.491 --> 00:08:15.940
is not a lot considering ITER program, the
massive internationally funded fusion generator

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:22.000
being built in France at the moment, estimates
a commercial reactor will need 300 grams of

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.169
tritium every day to generate 800 MegaWatts
of electrical power.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:33.050
Meaning we would eat through the entire global
supply of tritium in just over 2 months.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:38.530
[6] 800 megawatts is enough to cover about
2% of France’s peak power consumption.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:43.390
Even if we could continue sourcing Tritium
from nuclear fission reactors, they only produce

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:45.680
about 100 grams each per year.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:46.680
[7]

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:50.930
This is a major problem, however we do have
a solution in mind.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:56.990
We can use the high energy neutrons spit out
from the fusion reactions to do a bit of alchemy

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:58.190
wizardry.

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:03.360
When those high energy neutrons encounter
lithium, they can split the lithium into tritium

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.670
and helium. [7] Providing a steady supply
of tritium right where it’s needed.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:12.790
This is done in what is called a blanket around
the fusion chamber.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:17.720
The design of the blanket is one of the most
challenging parts of Tokamak fusion generator.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:25.110
ITER will test over 180 design variants of
this blanket that will line the donut shaped

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.110
interior.

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:30.630
Because, the blanket needs to do a lot more
than just breed tritium.

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.930
It is also where the energy of the fusion
reactions gets converted to heat.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:40.520
80% of the energy of the tritium deuterium
fusion reaction is carried away by those high

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:44.399
energy neutrons in the form of kinetic energy.

00:09:44.399 --> 00:09:47.720
We need a way to convert that kinetic energy
to electricity.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:48.720
[8]

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.440
As the fusion reaction rages in the centre
of the magnetically confined plasma, neutrons

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:59.260
begin to erupt outwards, unaffected by the
magnetic field thanks to their neutral magnetic

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:00.750
charge.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:06.140
Tokamaks convert the energy of these tiny
particles by slowing them down in the blanket,

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:10.700
trading their kinetic energy with atoms in
the blanket to heat energy.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.920
This heat energy is then captured by high
pressure water being pumped through cooling

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:20.150
channels, converting it to high pressure steam
to drive a steam turbine.

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:24.230
Humanities tried and tested method of creating
electricity.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:28.300
The material that fulfils this role needs
some other unique properties.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:34.040
First, in order to optimise for heating AND
tritium breeding, we need the material to

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:35.529
be a neutron multiplier.

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:41.090
When the high energy neutron from the fusion
reaction enters the blanket wall we want it

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:46.830
to strike an atom inside the blanket, and
release 2 neutrons.

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:51.650
Creating an additional neutron that allows
the blanket to fulfil both roles of generating

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.250
heat AND tritium.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:57.750
Beryllium is currently the leading candidate
for this role.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:02.512
When the neutron strikes it, it splits into
two helium 4 atoms and 2 neutrons.

00:11:02.512 --> 00:11:04.280
Multiplying our first neutron and allowing
our blanket to generate tritium and more heat.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:08.910
Beryllium, the same material used for the
James Webb Telescope mirrors, is the material

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:15.529
of choice because the helium byproduct does
not contaminate the plasma, and critically,

00:11:15.529 --> 00:11:19.290
the material retains little tritium within
itself.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:23.709
We need the tritium to naturally escape the
metal, partially because we need to collect

00:11:23.709 --> 00:11:30.500
the gas to replenish our fuel, but also because
tritium is explosive, just like normal hydrogen.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:31.500
.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.260
However, Beryllium does have its problems.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:39.060
First, the sheer quantity of beryllium a commercial
fusion reactor will require.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:44.459
Current designs call for between 216 to 560
tonnes.

00:11:44.459 --> 00:11:50.250
This is an issue because beryllium is extremely
expensive, due to the limited supply of the

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.760
material.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.329
Annual global supply last year amounted to
only 260 tonnes.

00:11:56.329 --> 00:12:02.180
The entire annual global supply of beryllium
could just about build one generator.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.600
Next, there are safety issues.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.970
Beryllium can contain large quantities of
uranium.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:12.959
China’s beryllium blanket module contains
100 parts per million uranium.

00:12:12.959 --> 00:12:18.310
So, 0.01 percent of the blanket is composed
of uranium.

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:22.570
This isn’t an issue for most components
that are usually made out of beryllium.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:27.750
Like the beryllium, aluminium, copper engine
pistons that were banned from Formula 1 in

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:28.750
2001.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:33.640
However, it becomes a massive problem when
the uranium is exposed to those high energy

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:34.640
neutrons.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:38.860
The same kind of neutrons that split uranium
in fission reactors.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.920
This creates fissile isotopes, or, in other
words, it makes the beryllium radioactive.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:44.920
[9]

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:49.290
If there were 30 parts per million of uranium
to beryllium, in a commercial scale fusion

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:57.480
reactor, that would mean there is 17 kilograms
of natural uranium and 123 grams of Uranium

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.230
235, the uranium needed for fission reactors.

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:05.650
The byproducts of this ura nium would make
disposing of the blanket at the end of the

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:08.949
generator's life incredibly difficult.[9]

00:13:08.949 --> 00:13:15.029
This all points to one major problem that
I see with Tokamak fusion reactors.

00:13:15.029 --> 00:13:20.220
Even if we manage to reach net energy output,
these generators don’t solve the biggest

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:23.290
problem holding nuclear energy back.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.300
Cost.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:28.160
Nuclear fission power is a wonder technology
of the last century.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.490
It promised abundant, clean, cheap electricity.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:36.470
A technology that we scarcely even dreamed
of 2 centuries ago, as we first discovered

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.670
the existence of the atom.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:44.970
Yet, we are closing down nuclear fission reactors
all across the world when we need that clean

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:50.060
power more than ever, because it’s uneconomical.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:55.050
The cost of building a nuclear fission reactor,
and dealing with the radioactive byproducts

00:13:55.050 --> 00:14:01.750
when decommissioning it are two primary factors
making it uneconomical, and Tokamak reactors

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:06.699
are driving straight towards the exact same
economic problem.

00:14:06.699 --> 00:14:10.570
However, one company is doing it differently,
Helion.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:17.160
The company I visited to witness nuclear fusion
reactions and interview their brilliant CEO

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:18.690
David Kirtley.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:23.750
They are doing things completely differently
to everyone else in nuclear fusion research.

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:28.940
They aren’t capturing energy with steam
power, eliminating the need for costly beryllium

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:30.180
blankets.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:35.870
They are developing a method of making fuel
on site that doesn’t require lithium, instead

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:40.780
using the cheap and plentiful deuterium to
create it during fusion.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:46.310
And they are using a completely different
magnetic confinement method to achieve nuclear

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:47.680
fusion temperatures.

